   216	
   217	There was nothing in Cael that had done it. He knew that then and he would know it afterward, whatever else he did not know. Nothing had leaned toward a door, and nothing had gone out of him. He had not reached for anything; he had been looking at the next half-second, and the next half-second had turned out to have a man in it whose read was pointed at an empty piece of floor.
   218	
   219	He was one short step away, in plain sight, with lamplight on him.
   220	
   221	He took the step, and put his left hand under Brom's lifted arm into the ribs, the short straight strike from the hip with nothing in it but a boy.
   222	
   223	It went in.
   224	
