     1	# Chapter 29 — Listening
     2	
     3	He spent most of the night looking for the edges of it, and found that it had none.
     4	
     5	He lay on his back in the dark with the blanket up to his chin and the frost growing on the window, and turned his attention outward, onto his own skin, where the new thing lay. He could feel that it was there. That was the strange part: he could feel it the way you feel a coat you have been wearing all day, not as weight exactly, only as the knowledge that something is on you. It lay over his arms and his back and his face, very thin, and it did not move, and it did not answer.
