    48	
    49	"And then?"
    50	
    51	"And then about the third week I stopped being angry, because you can't keep it up that long with nothing to feed it. I stopped listening for him to call me. I stopped listening for anything." Brom was quiet a moment. "That was the morning I first felt one of the gardeners on the other side of the wall. Through the brick. An old man with a barrow, and I could feel him going along, his weight and his tiredness and the barrow, like a man upstairs. I didn't know what it was. I nearly got up and ran."
    52	
    53	"He was teaching you."
    54	
    55	"He was teaching me. The old way. The way the houses do it, I found out after, in a book in the river city that cost me a week's purse to read." Brom closed the hand. "He was the only one in that whole house who did a single thing about what I could actually do. He never said so. He let me hate him for a month and never told me." He looked up at Cael. "I didn't work it out till the boat shed. Two years too late to thank him."
    56	
    57	Cael sat with that.
    58	
    59	He was thinking, for no reason he could at first find, of the mill at home, and the morning by the wheel-house when Hesk had put his small hand flat on the great wooden shaft and said *now watch the water*. He had stood there with his palm on the wood, doing nothing, waiting, and felt nothing for so long he thought he was being teased. And then the knock had come up through the shaft into his hand, and the whole slow strength of the river with it. Hesk had not told him to feel for it. Hesk had told him to watch the water and keep his hand where it was.
    60	
    61	*Like a hand on a beam*, Brom had said, in the alcove, about the read. *You feel who's moving on the floor above.*
    62	
    63	It was the same hand: a boy's hand on a shaft and a man's hand on a beam and a boy of fourteen with his back against a garden wall. None of them were reaching; they were all only resting there, flat, on something that carried weight, and letting the weight come.
    64	
    65	The alcove was quiet, and out on the floor the sweepers had begun, the long sound of the brooms.
    66	
    67	"You can't listen and talk at once," said Brom. "That's the whole of it, I think. That's what the wall was for. And everything you've got is talking. The step's talking. The thing in your arm's talking. The way you watch people, the hooks, that's the loudest of all; it's you asking them questions as fast as they can answer." He sat back. "This doesn't ask. You don't send it anywhere. You leave the door open and sit down, and whatever's out there walks in when it likes."
    68	
    69	---
    70	
    71	Getting quiet enough turned out to be the hardest thing anybody had ever asked him to do.
    72	
    73	He had been quiet all his life, on the outside. He could sit on a bench for six hours and not move. But it was a hunter's quiet, a quiet with a question in the middle of it, and the question never stopped. He sat on the alcove floor with his legs crossed and his hands on his knees and tried to stop asking, and found that his attention would not be still.
    74	
    75	It was a trained thing, and he had spent a year training it. Asked to point at nothing and wait, it twitched and went looking, like a dog that has been taught to fetch, and every time it went looking, it was aiming again, and Brom on the bench said, quietly, "There," and Cael knew he had lost it before he had found it.
    76	
    77	There turned out to be a great many ways to fail at doing nothing.
    78	
    79	He failed by counting his own breath, because counting was a thing he did, and a count was a question. He failed by listening hard for Brom, which was aiming. He failed by deciding not to listen for Brom, and then listening to find out whether he was listening. He failed by beginning, without meaning to, to go down into the hooks on Brom's resting body, *dormant, dormant*, as he had for twelve days from that same bench. Once he failed by noticing that his left knee hurt, and then by noticing that he was noticing, and then by laughing at himself, which Brom said was the closest he had come all morning.
    80	
    81	He lost count of the failures somewhere past thirty. Brom did not. Brom said, at one point, "That's forty," in the voice of a man reading the number off a slate, and Cael said something short and unkind about the number forty, and Brom's mouth went in at the corner.
    82	
    83	After the fortieth he stopped trying. He did not decide to; there was nothing left to try with. He was tired all the way down, and his head ached from the gaze, and his knees ached from the floor, and he let his hands lie open on them and thought, for no reason, about lying on his back on the main floor a week ago with his legs gone, looking up at the lamps.
    84	
    85	He had had nothing to do then; that was what he remembered. The bout was over and he was not getting up. There had been nothing left in the world to aim at, and so for the length of three breaths he had simply lain there and looked at the field of small flames on the low ceiling, and the dark beams running across it, and let them be there.
    86	
    87	He let himself be there again.
    88	
    89	And something came in.
    90	
    91	It was not subtle, and that was what shocked him afterward, when he could think. He had expected something faint, something he would have to strain after, a whisper at the edge of things, and it was not a whisper.
    92	
    93	Three paces in front of him, on the bench, there was a man, and Cael could feel him the way you feel a fire at your back when you are standing in a cold room with your coat to it: all at once, without turning round, exactly where it is. It pressed on the front of him. It was big, and warm, and steady, and alive, alive in a way that nothing else in the alcove was, the wall and the post and the lamp all gone dim and flat beside it.
    94	
    95	And it had a shape. He could not have said what the shape was; he had no words yet. But it was the same shape he had felt in the first breath of the notice, the great blurred weight like a door; and it was also, he understood with a jolt that nearly lost him the whole of it, the thing that had been laid on him for a month. *This* was what Brom had been doing at the end of the east bench, at the rope, a bench's length off across the alcove. Cael was feeling the hand that had been feeling him.
    96	
    97	He held it. One breath. Two. He knew, without any measuring, which way it lay from him, and that it was close; how close, he could not tell, the distance smeared like a lamp seen through steam. Three breaths. Four.
    98	
    99	Brom shifted his weight on the bench.
   100	
   101	It was a small movement, nothing, a big man settling. But Cael's attention went to it. It went the way it had been trained to go, toward the movement, quick, to find out what it meant; and the moment it went, it was aiming, and the fire at his back was gone, and he was sitting on a cold floor in a lamplit alcove with his eyes wide open and his heart going like a hammer.
   102	
