   152	
   153	He went out, and Cael heard his steps go across the main floor, and the side door open, and the cold come in, and the door shut.
   154	
   155	He sat on the alcove floor a long time with the band round his head and his hands open on his knees, and did not try to feel anything at all.
   156	
   157	After a while the sweepers came to the arch with their brooms and looked at him, a boy sitting cross-legged on the stone in an empty alcove with his eyes half shut, and decided among themselves without a word to do that corner last. He heard them go on along the wall. He heard the side door, and somebody calling for the chestnut man, and the first of the noon newcomers coming down the floor from Keth's ring, the tall lad laughing at something, all of it ordinary and far away.
   158	
   159	He thought that he had spent a year learning to see everything. He had sat on the east bench with his eyes doing the work of a whole body, and been proud of what they found, and paid for it in aches and a blind spot ten degrees wide. And now there was a thing lying on his skin that did not want him to look at anything at all. It wanted him to be the floor, and let the weight come down on him, and say what it was.
   160	
   161	He did not know whether he could do that. He did not know anybody who had ever asked it of him before, except a man in a lit alcove with a garden wall in his past, and an old man at home with his hand on a mill shaft. He found he was smiling, alone, on the cold stone, with his head aching.
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	He wrote it that evening at the crate desk, with the lamp turned low because of his head, and the Power Log open to the new entry. He had begun it the morning before, in ink, with the notice copied at the head; now he gave it the fields, all six, in the order he would never change. He did it slowly, and plainly, and left out nothing he had been given, and nothing he had paid.
   166	
   167	*IRON-ADJACENT.*
   168	
   169	*What it is: the edge of B.'s read. A thin thing on the skin, all over, outside. It doesn't go out. Things come in.*
   170	
   171	*Source: B. Third morning, mid-reset, not asked for. Ruling: same road as the other two. Close, and long, and a real person, and not taken. Found afterward.*
   172	
   173	*Conditions: still. Quiet. Not trying. The moment I aim at it, it shuts. Ruling: I have to stop asking before it answers. I'm worst at that of anything.*
   174	
   175	*Deployment range: one strong one, close, three paces or under. Opened once in about forty tries, then twice more. Held four breaths, three, five. Lost every time he moved, or I did. Gives: where, which way, that he's alive, a shape I haven't words for. Doesn't give: how far, exactly. Who. Anything moving. Anything at range.*
   176	
   177	*Costs: attention itself, whatever I look with. Four breaths cost what a night of the gaze costs. A band round the head a minute after, an hour or more. And it won't share: if the hip leans or the hollow stirs, it goes. Opening it means putting both of the others down.*
   178	
   179	*Open questions: Two people in a room. A crowd. Somebody who isn't kindled. Whether it gets cheaper, like his. Whether it ever runs while I move. What it's for, if it isn't for anything I point. B.: it's for knowing.*
   180	
   181	He read the entry through: it was the shortest *Deployment range* in the book and the longest *Open questions*. That was right; that was what the book was supposed to look like.
   182	
   183	There was one more thing he made himself write, because he could feel himself wanting to fold Tuesday into it. The new thing had come from Brom's read, and on Tuesday Brom's read had lost him, and the two lay side by side in his head as neatly as a lock and a key. That neatness was the reason not to trust it. He turned back to Tuesday's page, to the third exchange, and set down beside it, in pencil, only what he had seen of each.
   184	
   185	*Not the same thing. This came with a hush and a notice; the third came with neither. This lies on my skin and I can feel it lying there; the third left nothing on me to feel, before or after. This takes something in, for a few breaths, a man as big as a door; in the third I took nothing in. I was looking at his shoulder. This costs a band round the head; the third cost nothing I can find. This opens only when I'm still and stop aiming; the third came while I was moving, in the middle of an exchange. This is a piece of his read. The third was his read finding nobody there. One I have and can't use yet. The other I never had, and can't find. It stays where it fell.*
   186	
   187	Then he turned forward to the new entry again and wrote at the bottom, outside the fields, because there was one more true thing and he wanted it said:
   188	
   189	*I own something I cannot use. Twice before I've owned something I couldn't use, and both times the road from owning to using was a winter of mornings like this one, on stone, getting it wrong in front of somebody patient. I know that road now. I know what it costs a day. Begin.*
   190	
   191	He capped the ink.
   192	
   193	He sat a while with his hand on the closed book. His head had eased to a dull pressure. Through the wall he could hear Lira moving about her room, not yet asleep, not yet gone down the hill to her lamp.
   194	
   195	He let it drift open without meaning to. He was only sitting very still, tired, with nothing to aim at, and the thing on his skin found the stillness on its own and opened, a crack. And there through the wall, two paces off, faint, there was somebody: a light quick weight, never still for long, like a bird on a sill that keeps shifting its feet.
   196	
   197	He shut it.
   198	
   199	He shut it hard, the way you shut a letter you have found open on somebody's table and realise is not yours. The band came back round his head for his trouble. He sat with it and did not mind it.
   200	
   201	Then he opened the Log again, to Tuesday's page, to the narrow empty column at the edge headed *Carrying*, and after a while he wrote the first thing in it, very small.
   202	
   203	*L., through the wall. Didn't.*
   204	
   205	He would show her, since she had asked him to show her first.
   206	
   207	Down at the bottom of the hill, through the window over the alcoves, the practice lamp came on, and he watched it for a while.
   208	
   209	On Monday Dace would chalk the main season on the wall by the slate, and the floors would fill, and somebody would want him on their card who had not wanted him a week ago. He had three things that were not a Path now, and one of them he could not use in any room with more than one person in it. He had a friend whose whole Path was knowing where people stood, and who still had nowhere to put him. He had a partner at a lamp in the north window who had not yet decided what she thought of that friend, and was entitled to take her time.
   210	
   211	He blew out his own lamp, and lay down, and let the cold room be a cold room, and slept.

