     1	# Chapter 18 — Stimulus, Latency, Response
     2	
     3	The district's reading room was over the dyers' hall at the bottom of the market steps, up a stair that smelled of indigo and wet wool, and Cael had been in it perhaps a dozen times in a year.
     4	
     5	It was one long room under the roof with a stove at one end and a locked press at the other. Between them stood four tables, where men in fingerless gloves sat reading old notices and almanacs and the shipping lists from the river. Anybody could sit at the tables for nothing. The books in the press cost a copper each to have out, and the copper was the least of it. The keeper of the room, a thin old man with ink on both his cuffs, wrote your name in a book of his own beside the number of whatever you had taken, and kept the book forever.
     6	
     7	Cael stood at the press with his copper in his hand and looked at the old man's book, open on its slanted desk, with the pen beside it.
     8	
     9	Everything he gave them went in a file somewhere. He had written that on the officials page in his own hand. This was not the Compact, only an old man in a room over a dye works; but a name was a name, and the book was a book, and Cael had been fourteen in a hall with a dark Arbiter over his head and had learned there exactly how long a thing written down could last.
    10	
    11	"Name," said the old man.
    12	
    13	"Hesk-ward."
    14	
    15	The old man wrote it, slowly and well, and did not look up, and gave him the key.
    16	
    17	He took down everything in the press that so much as mentioned Iron Skin, which was three books. There was a big brown register of the Paths of the realm, a hundred years old and re-bound twice, and a smaller book on the defensive Paths in particular, and a guild almanac from a city up the coast with a chapter on the families who trained them. He carried all three to the table nearest the stove and sat with them through the whole of the cold afternoon.
    18	
    19	The register was laid out the way the Compact laid out everything, in rows. He had read its front pages before, in the spring, when he was trying to understand the ladder he had been put at the bottom of. There was the table of the metals, Copper and Iron and Bronze and Silver and Gold, with ten ranks to each and a line under the tenth where the station had to look at you again. There was the list of what each metal let you do and where it let you go: Copper the outer districts and the limited market; Iron the whole city, and the guild district with your credentials in your hand; Bronze the administrative core as well, where the gates were. And after the front pages came the Paths themselves, each one a row, with its branch and the metals at which anybody had ever been recorded holding it, and a line or two of what it did. There was no row in it for anybody who had not been given a Path, and he had stopped looking for one a long time ago.
    20	
    21	The register gave Iron Skin a line among the defensive Paths, between a Path for turning blows and another for standing, and said it hardened the body against force. It had been recorded at every metal from Copper to Gold, and at none above. The book on defensive Paths gave it a little more, and all of it was the same thing said at greater length. The almanac gave it four pages.
    22	
    23	He read the four pages twice, because he did not at first believe them. They were about who owned it. Here were the three guild houses that taught it, and when each had been founded and by whom. Here was which of them had quarrelled with which, and which families had sent their children to which house for four generations, and what those children had become. One page was nothing but a list of marriages. And in the middle of the second page, between the founding of one house and the founding of the next, there was a single sentence on what the Path did. *Its practitioners raise a hardness of the surface of the body at need, the art of which is the property of the houses named above.*
    24	
    25	He copied the sentence out exactly into the grey book. Under it he wrote: *Four pages on who owns it. One sentence on what it does. The registry's the same, only shorter.*
    26	
    27	He sat a while looking at the line.
    28	
    29	He had thought, coming up the dyers' stair, that he was going to read about a Path. He found he had read about a door instead, and the people who kept the key to it, and how long they had kept it. That was what the whole ladder was, he supposed, if you looked at it from far enough off: Copper, Iron, Bronze, and the guild letter, and the station, and a book in a hall with your name in it. It was four pages on who owned you and a sentence on what you could do.
    30	
    31	He thought of Lira at the top of Copper, at ten, with the door in front of her that only a guild's letter would open, and of the six guilds she had asked and the two halls she had walked to. He thought of the almanac's page of marriages. Somewhere at the back of the reading room the old man with the ink on his cuffs coughed and turned a page of his own book, the one with *Hesk-ward* in it now, and Cael found he did not mind it as much as he had at the press. An old man's book over a dye works was at least honest about what it was for. It wanted to know who had the key.
    32	
    33	He went to a second reading room the next afternoon, a cold one in the old chapel by the north gate, on the theory that a room nobody used might have older books. It had older books, and none of them said anything the first room's had not. After two days of rooms he stopped going to them.
    34	
    35	---
    36	
    37	He tried people next, which was harder, because people wanted to know why you were asking, and in the Ironyard everybody already knew.
    38	
    39	Corrin found him rather than the other way round. He was a big grey Stone fighter with a face like a split log, who had been fighting in the Ironyard since before Dace kept the slate. He no longer fought much, but he sat on the end of the front bench most nights with his hands on his stick and told whoever sat next to him what was wrong with the bout in front of them. He stopped Cael at the water barrel on the second evening.
    40	
    41	"You're asking round about Iron Skin."
    42	
    43	"I am."
    44	
    45	"Because of the big one." Corrin did not wait for an answer. "I fought one once. Years back, in a port up the coast, before your mother had you. A woman, not big. Iron Skin, Iron tier, and she looked at me like I was a horse she was thinking of buying." He worked his right hand open and shut on the head of his stick. The knuckles were thick, and two of them did not close all the way. "I'll tell you what it's like and you can make of it what you want. It's not her hitting you. She hardly hit me at all. It's your own hit. You put your best into her, everything you've got, and the hit comes back up your arm the wrong way, on a line you didn't send it on. Your wrist knows before your eyes do. Like punching water that decided, halfway, to be a wall."
    46	
    47	Cael wrote it down while the old man watched him do it.
    48	
    49	"Did you beat her?"
    50	
    51	"I did not." Corrin seemed to find this funny. "She stood there. I kept hitting her. Every time, it came back, a little worse. By the fourth I couldn't make a fist." He opened and shut the hand again. "Took a year to come right, and it never came all the way."
    52	
    53	"How did she stand?"
    54	
    55	Corrin looked at him with some respect, as if it were a better question than he had expected.
    56	
    57	"Like a gatepost. Back. Way back, on her heels, like somebody'd planted her and come back in a year to see if she'd taken." He snorted. "I thought it was a fault. I thought, she can't go anywhere from there. I was going to make her chase me. She didn't chase. Why would she?"
    58	
    59	"Did she ever start anything? Strike first?"
