     3	He woke before the alarm he hadn't set, the way he always did on days that mattered, and spent the first several minutes of the morning doing the thing he always did before he let himself get out of bed: cataloguing.
     4	
     5	Ceiling. The room's familiar shape, Lira's room still silent on the other side of the wall. His own body, run through in order, the inventory a year of circuit fighting had made automatic: legs rested and full, the deep reserve topped up by two easy days; the left wrist's old complaint quiet; breath slow and organized, four counts in, six out. The morning's quiet hadn't decided yet whether it belonged to nerves or readiness — he'd learned, across a year of mornings like this one, that the two feelings arrived wearing nearly identical clothes and the only real way to tell them apart was to get up and start moving.
     6	
     7	He ran through the transition-window hypothesis one final time before breakfast, not because rehearsing it again would sharpen it any further — two weeks of watching had already given the theory whatever shape it was going to have — but because the rehearsal itself had become part of how he readied himself for anything that mattered. Third exchange. If the window existed at all, it would show itself there, when Brom was most settled into pattern and least braced for something to interrupt it.
     8	
     9	He ran the other rehearsal too, the one he'd added this time and told no one about: the discipline plan for his own fragments. Wind-adjacent rationed — bursts only off a read, never off a reaction, because reactive bursts spent from the reserve and the reserve had to last four exchanges against a man who would spend nothing. Pressure-adjacent locked away entirely. He'd written the rule three days ago and underlined it: *no amplification against a hardened surface, no matter how good the opening looks. The better the opening looks, the more it will cost when it comes back.* He suspected, with the honesty he tried to apply to himself as rigorously as to opponents, that the rule would be hardest to keep at exactly the moment it mattered most — mid-fight, behind on the score, with a clean line to Brom's jaw and a year of trained instinct screaming to take it with everything available. That was why it was written down. Rules you only kept when they were easy weren't rules.
