    72	
    73	The second line had a reason attached, and the reason was standing across the circle from him with his weight distributed so evenly that Cael's eye kept sliding off it, looking for the lean that wasn't there. Pressure-adjacent was impact amplification. Against most opponents, more force meant more result. Against a surface that hardened at the instant of contact and handed the force back, more force meant a larger donation. Every amplified strike he threw at Brom would come home with interest, at an angle of Brom's choosing. So the reserve stayed shut. Whatever happened tonight, it stayed shut.
    74	
    75	Which left the hypothesis. Two weeks of watching Brom work the training posts in the side alcoves, session after session, and Cael had seen — or believed he'd seen — a seam. A softness in the Iron Skin between its two modes: the defensive set that met an incoming strike, and the offensive set that turned his own blows into something that arrived twice. A transition. A window. If it existed, it would open under alternation — if Cael could force the Iron Skin to switch modes fast enough, often enough, there would be a fraction of a second when it was neither, and a fraction of a second was a working space he'd fitted himself into before.
