    87	By the fourth contact he understood the shape of his error, and it was an old error, the kind he filed with special care because it came from good procedure honestly executed. Two weeks of observation, thirty feet away, watching Brom work the posts — and what he'd read as a lag in the Iron Skin, a softness before the set, had been the post. Wood flexing under load. The give had belonged to the equipment, not the man. Live, at the length of his own arm, with real intent behind the contact, the transition from flesh to iron was not fast. It was not anything. There was no interval in it to be fast across. The window he had built two weeks of planning on did not exist, and had never existed, and the fifth contact confirmed it, and the sixth put the confirmation through his elbow hard enough to numb the forearm to the wrist.
