   266	
   267	He'd come to Ardenmere with a bag and a direction and nothing beyond that. A year later, he had a district that recognized him, a ledger with his name written into it in someone else's careful hand, and now, apparently, a friend who liked him precisely because he couldn't be solved. It was not the life he'd imagined lying awake in Denvash the night before his Kindling. He found, turning it over one last time before sleep finally won, that he didn't mind the difference at all.
   268	
   269	He closed the notebook, set it beside the bed, and slept better than the aching in his legs had any right to let him.
   270	
