     1	# Chapter 12 — What Brom Knows
     2	
     3	He'd woken sore in a way that had nothing to do with the previous day's fight and everything to do with the deeper exhaustion of a body that had spent four exchanges compensating for compromised legs — not the sharp, immediate ache of a fresh injury, but the duller kind that settled in overnight and made itself known the moment he tried to swing his feet off the cot. He took the inventory lying down, because standing wasn't yet a settled question: thighs and calves reporting the double spending of damage plus four bursts, a bone-deep protest that flared when he flexed either foot; the strange, unspent fullness under the ribs already faded overnight, the Pressure fragment's reservoir apparently indifferent to whether its contents were used or merely carried; and underneath both, at rest, the two familiar presences he'd stopped consciously noticing months ago and noticed freshly now, the way an injury makes you re-notice a limb. Wind-adjacent sat in the legs even when the legs were ruined — a coiled readiness, patient, like a held breath that belonged to someone else. Pressure-adjacent sat lower and heavier, a stone in still water. Neither asked anything of him. They were simply resident, in the manner of things that had long since stopped being visitors.
     4	
     5	He'd taken the morning slowly because of it, later than his usual pace to the market, favoring one side without quite thinking about it.
