    87	
    88	"Good." Something settled into his expression that Cael would come to recognize, over the following months, as Brom's own version of genuine delight — not a smile exactly, more a kind of stillness that meant he'd found a problem worth keeping. "That means you get to choose the word."
    89	
    90	"What if I choose badly?"
    91	
    92	"Then you choose again later. Words aren't fixed the way Paths are supposed to be. You get to keep revising until it fits." He said this with the flat certainty of someone who'd clearly thought about the question longer than the casual delivery suggested. "My family thought Iron Skin at Copper meant a fixed, disappointing word. I decided the word wasn't fixed. Turns out I was right and they were wrong. I expect it'll go the same way for you, on a far larger scale."
    93	
    94	They sat with that for a while, the alcove's morning light shifting slightly as the sun climbed, the Ironyard beyond the wall beginning its ordinary drift toward midday noise.
    95	
    96	"Does it change anything?" Cael asked, eventually. "Knowing all of it now. The full shape."
    97	
    98	Brom considered the question with real care, as he considered most things that weren't simply reflex. "It makes me more interested, not less. I told you that already." He paused. "I think it also makes me more careful. Not of you — for you. Three fragments in a year is fast. Fast usually means either something is very wrong or something is very unusual, and I've watched enough people mistake the second for the first to want to be sure before I decide which one this is."
    99	
   100	"You don't think it's wrong."
   101	
   102	"I don't know yet. I think it's early. I think you should keep the log the way you're keeping it — precise, honest, more interested in the true answer than the comfortable one — and I think that's probably the best protection either of us has against getting it wrong." He stood, offering Cael a hand up, the sparring session apparently concluded early for the day by unspoken agreement. "Same time tomorrow?"
   103	
   104	"Same time tomorrow."
   105	
   106	---
   107	
   108	Tomorrow, as it turned out, was when the humbling started.
   109	
   110	Brom arrived with an agenda he announced from the alcove's entrance, before he'd even set his things down: "New arrangement for today. We're not sparring. You're going to try to use it, and I'm going to watch you fail, and then we're going to figure out why you're failing. I've spent four years learning what that component is supposed to do. I'm the closest thing to an instructor the fragment is ever going to have. We'd be stupid not to use that."
   111	
   112	Cael had no objection. He'd spent half the night lying awake with his attention turned outward toward the new resident, trying to find its edges, and had learned exactly one thing: it was there. Present the way the others were present, a thin distributed film of potential laid across his skin, waiting. What it was waiting *for*, he couldn't find. Wind-adjacent had come with a natural handle — the step wanted legs, and legs he understood. Pressure-adjacent had wanted rhythm and a strike to ride. This one wanted something he didn't have a name for, and every attempt to simply *will* it into function slid off it like a hand on wet glass.
   113	
   114	The first hour produced nothing at all. Brom stood three paces away, pressure-read fully active, generating — at Cael's request — the strongest, steadiest signature he could produce, a target Brom described as "standing in front of a lit lamp and asking you to notice the light." Cael noticed nothing. He tried reaching for the fragment as he reached for the step — a demand, sharp, timed — and got silence. He tried the Pressure fragment's approach, settling into rhythm and inviting it to join, and got silence. He stood, sat, closed his eyes, held his breath, and got silence, and by the end of the hour the silence had acquired the texture of a locked door being knocked on by someone who owned the house.
   115	
   116	"You're commanding it," Brom said, finally, from his post. "I can see you commanding it. Your whole body does the thing it does before a strike — organizes, aims, executes. That's backwards. The read isn't a strike. It's not even an action, properly. It's a *posture.* You don't send it out. You get quiet enough that things come in." He paused, visibly searching his own body for language that had never needed to be language before. "When my instructors first trained the sensitivity, they made me sit against the estate wall for a month doing nothing. I thought it was punishment. It was the entire lesson. You can't listen and speak at the same time, and everything you've built — the step, the amplification, the whole way you fight — is speech."
   117	
   118	It was, Cael would write later, the single most useful piece of instruction anyone had ever given him about his own architecture, and it still took the rest of the morning to apply. Getting quiet, it turned out, was a skill he had in abundance everywhere except inside his own body. His attention was a trained instrument that pointed at things; asked to point at nothing, to simply lie open and receive, it twitched back toward aiming like a compass needle finding north. He failed forty times in ways too small to describe. On roughly the forty-first, sitting cross-legged on the alcove floor with his eyes open and unfocused, breath run down to its slowest organized rhythm, he stopped trying to find Brom's signature — genuinely stopped, the internal equivalent of setting a tool down — and the signature walked in on its own.
   119	
   120	It was not subtle. That was the shock of it. Brom's pressure-read, three paces away, arrived against his new surface-sense like a hand pressed flat against his sternum — a weight, directional, unmistakably *alive*, textured in a way he had no vocabulary for yet but recognized instantly from the bout: this was what had been reading him. He was feeling the feeler. He held it for four full breaths, mapping as fast as the stillness allowed — direction clean, distance vague, intensity vivid — and then Brom shifted his weight, and Cael's attention twitched toward the movement, and the whole channel collapsed like a startled bird leaving a branch.
   121	
   122	The headache arrived about a minute later, a tight band behind both eyes, entirely unlike the honest muscular costs of the other two fragments. Wind spent his legs. Pressure spent his core. This spent something he could only locate as *attention itself* — four breaths of successful reading had drawn down the same reserve that a full morning of compound-gaze observation drew, compressed into seconds.
   123	
   124	"Well?" Brom asked.
   125	
   126	"I had you. Four breaths. Then you moved and I lost it." Cael pressed the heels of his hands briefly against his eyes. "And it costs. More than it should, for four breaths."
   127	
   128	"It costs me almost nothing. But I've been paying into it for four years — the price comes down with use. Mine did." Brom crossed the alcove and sat down across from him, close enough now that when Cael cautiously opened the surface-sense again, the signature nearly shouted. "Again. This time I'm going to move, slowly, and you're going to try to keep me."
   129	
   130	He couldn't. That was the afternoon's second lesson, delivered over two further hours of patient failure: the read, in its current infant state, survived only in stillness. The moment Cael moved — shifted weight, turned his head, so much as organized his body toward movement — the listening posture broke, displaced by the same trained readiness that made him useful in a fight. Worse: it wouldn't share. When he tried, experimentally, to hold the surface-sense open while priming the Wind-adjacent step, the two did not so much conflict as flatly displace one another, the step's gathering readiness shouldering the listening aside the instant it formed, one resident claiming the whole hallway. Brom, watching him try, offered the only consolation on offer: "The read runs under everything I do, but it took two years before it would run under movement. You're on day one. Stop grading day one against year four."
   131	
   132	By the session's end, Cael's total functional capability with his third fragment amounted to this: seated or standing still, in a quiet space, against a single strong signature at three paces or less, he could open the channel in about four breaths and hold it for five or six before either the target moved, his own attention twitched, or the draining band behind his eyes forced him to let go. Against the crowded, roaring conditions of an actual bout, it was as useless as a candle in daylight. He said so, out loud, because honesty about capability was the whole discipline.
   133	
   134	"Useless in a fight," Brom agreed, standing, gathering his things. "For now. So was I, the first year. My family's investment in the Path assumed a decade. You don't get a decade, so you'll practice the way you practice everything, which is more than anyone I've met. But hear the actual lesson of today, because it isn't about effort." He looked down at Cael, still seated, and said it plainly. "Every other thing you own is a weapon, and you learned it by aiming. This one will never once be aimed. If you try to make it a weapon, you'll build a bad copy of your other tools and miss what it's actually for. It's for *knowing.* You, of all people, should not need convincing that knowing is worth owning on its own terms."
   135	
   136	---
   137	
   138	Cael walked back to the boarding house alone that afternoon, Lira off running an errand of her own, the band behind his eyes finally loosening, and found himself turning the whole two days over as he did anything that had shifted the shape of what he understood about himself.
   139	
   140	He thought about Feryn, months ago now, asking the same essential question — *what tier are you* — and accepting *I don't know yet* as a complete answer, satisfied simply to have asked. He thought about Darrow, on the ground after the marquee bout, asking the identical question in different words. He thought, now, about Brom, who'd gone one step further than either of them: not just curious about the shape of the mystery, but actively invested in helping him solve it, offering his own Path as raw material for the solving without ever once suggesting that made him owed anything in return. There was no precedent in Cael's experience for what had happened in the alcove today — a practitioner spending an entire training day teaching someone else to use a stolen piece of his own inheritance, patiently, as though the theft were the most interesting gift he'd ever been given.
   141	
   142	It was, Cael understood, a different category of relationship than anything he'd built before Ardenmere — not the unconditional, foundational loyalty of Hesk, which had existed before Cael had done anything at all to earn it, and not quite the fierce, hard-won partnership he and Lira had built out of a year of shared risk and shared recovery. Something adjacent to both, built on curiosity rather than obligation, on two people who'd each decided, independently, that the other was worth understanding properly rather than simply tolerating.
   143	
   144	He wrote it into the observation notebook first, briefly, a tactical note about how Brom processed new information — carefully, without visible alarm, weighing a fact fully before reacting to it rather than reacting first and reconsidering later. It was, Cael thought, a genuinely useful thing to know about someone he expected to keep training beside for a long time.
   145	
   146	Then he opened the Power Log, and gave the third fragment the structured entry the first day's evidence had earned — the same format as the others, filled in with a day's honest failures:
   147	
   148	*Iron-adjacent — working ledger, day one.*
   149	
   150	*Gain: surface pressure read. Confirmed functional against a single strong signature at three paces, stationary, quiet conditions. What it returns: presence, direction, aliveness, some texture. Not yet: distance with precision, identity, anything at range, anything in motion.*
   151	
   152	*Cost: attention itself — the same reserve the compound gaze draws on, spent at many times the rate. A few breaths of reading produced an hour of headache. Secondary cost: exclusivity. It will not run alongside either other fragment yet. Opening it means setting everything else down.*
   153	
   154	*Useful where: reading one known subject under controlled conditions. Eventually — if Brom's arc is any guide — under movement, in noise, beneath the other abilities. Not yet.*
   155	
   156	*Fails where: everywhere else. Any movement of mine. Any startle of attention. Multiple signatures — untested, but I already know what a crowd will do to an open channel, because I know what a crowd does to unguarded listening of any kind. Test carefully. Small doses.*
   157	
   158	*Honest summary: I own something I cannot use. The last two times I owned something I couldn't use, the gap between owning and using was months of exactly this kind of day. Begin.*
   159	
   160	And beneath it, the entry that actually mattered:
   161	
   162	*Three fragments now. Wind-adjacent. Pressure-adjacent. Iron-adjacent. Brom knows all three — the full log, not a summary. He didn't flinch at any of it. He asked better questions about it than I've asked myself. Then he spent a full day teaching me to use the piece of himself I'd taken, and seemed to enjoy it.*
   163	
   164	*I keep expecting the people who matter to me to eventually reach the edge of their patience with how strange this is. Hesk hasn't. Lira hasn't. Now Brom hasn't either. I'm starting to wonder if that edge was something I invented to brace myself against a disappointment that keeps declining to actually arrive — a wound I built in advance, out of Denvash's reflexes, that Ardenmere has never once given me reason to reopen.*
   165	
   166	*Three fragments. One friend who reads pressure signatures for a living and still can't categorize mine. One partner who's known the whole shape of it since before I told her properly. A grandfather three days' letter away who's never once asked me to be smaller than whatever I turn out to be.*
   167	
   168	*I don't know what I am yet. I know, more clearly than I did a year ago, that I'm not carrying the finding-out alone.*
   169	
   170	He closed the log, and sat for a while in the quiet of the boarding house's common room — and caught himself, once, in the stillness, letting the new surface-sense drift halfway open just to feel the room's shape press faintly against it, the walls and the warm mass of the stove and somewhere above him a boarder's footsteps registering as a soft rhythmic weight. He let it close. He had months of failures ahead of him before it would be anything more than this. He found he didn't mind. He'd been here twice before, at the bottom of exactly this climb, and both times the climb itself had turned out to be where most of the understanding lived.
   171	
   172	Ardenmere had given him more than he'd had any right to expect from a district he'd arrived in with nothing but a bag, a notebook he couldn't yet open, and one direction to walk in. Tonight it had given him a third thing that wasn't a Path, a teacher it had never occurred to him to hope for, and a headache he'd earned honestly. He went to bed early, and slept, and did not dream of doors.
   173	
   174	---

