editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:7:He tried asking it the way he asked the Wind. A drop, sharp, all at once, the way the hip went. Nothing dropped, because there was nowhere in the coat to drop from. He tried the way of the giving face: found a beat in his breathing, slow, and let it run, and waited for the new thing to come up and ride on it. Nothing rode. He tried simply wanting it, hard, with his jaw set, which had never worked on anything in his life and did not work now. Every time he leaned on it his attention slid off, like a hand on a frosted window, and he was lying in a cold room in the dark with his eyes open, being a fool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:9:Toward morning he stopped, and lay there with his hands on the blanket, and thought about the Wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:17:Brom was in the alcove before him, for once. He had the lamp lit and his coat on its nail and his hands not wound, and he was standing in the middle of the stone with his arms hanging and his weight back, looking at nothing. When Cael came in at the arch he did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:19:"No crosses today," he said. "Nobody's hitting anybody. You're going to stand there, or sit, whatever you like, and try to find me with it. I'm going to make it easy." He did something then that Cael could not see, and did not need to see. "I'm as loud as I go. I'm standing here with the read open all the way and nothing else in my head. If you can't hear me now, you won't hear anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:29:Three paces away a man stood with the read open as far as it went, and Cael, who had a piece of that same read lying on every inch of his skin, could not feel him any more than he could feel the moon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:31:By the end of the hour the not-feeling had a shape of its own. It was like standing in an empty yard calling a dog by its name, knowing the dog was somewhere close, and that it was his, and that it was not going to come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:43:"Then what does it do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:47:"I said at the wall I'd tell you the rest of the estate wall," he said. "The yard man. The month of sitting." He turned the hand over. "I thought he'd put me there to be out of the way. While they talked about me in the long room. I hated him for it. Every morning for a month, after the station, he sat me down on the flags with my back to the wall of the kitchen garden and said *stay there till I call you*, and went in, and left me. No post. No falls. Nothing. Just the wall, and the cold, and the gardeners going by with their barrows. I was fourteen and I'd just been read Copper and everybody in the house had gone quiet, and I sat there with nothing to do but be angry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:55:"He was teaching me. The old way. The way the houses do it, I found out after, in a book in the river city that cost me a week's purse to read." Brom closed the hand. "He was the only one in that whole house who did a single thing about what I could actually do. He never said so. He let me hate him for a month and never told me." He looked up at Cael. "I didn't work it out till the boat shed. Two years too late to thank him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:61:*Like a hand on a beam*, Brom had said, in the alcove, about the read. *You feel who's moving on the floor above.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:81:He lost count of the failures somewhere past thirty. Brom did not. Brom said, at one point, "That's forty," in the voice of a man reading the number off a slate, and Cael said something short and unkind about the number forty, and Brom's mouth went in at the corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:97:He held it. One breath. Two. He knew, without any measuring, which way it lay from him, and that it was close; how close, he could not tell, the distance smeared like a lamp seen through steam. Three breaths. Four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:113:It came about a minute after, quite suddenly, as if it had been waiting politely at the door. It was not like the eye-ache of the gaze, which lived behind his forehead and grew slowly through a night. It was a band, and it went round his head from temple to temple behind his eyes and pulled tight, and stayed tight, and the alcove lamp became a thing he did not want to look at. He sat with his eyes shut and his palms cupped over them, keeping the lamp out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:115:He knew what it was: the same thing the gaze spent, the same thing that went out of him on a long night on the east bench and came back with sleep; only the gaze took a whole bout to draw that much, and this had drawn it in four breaths. Four breaths of feeling the man had cost him what an evening of watching cost. The Wind spent his hip and his breath. The giving face spent the hollow under his ribs. This spent something he could only call his attention itself, the plain stuff he did all his looking with, and it spent it the way a crack in a jug spends water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:129:He found that out by trying, near the end, when he was tired and stupid and the band had settled into a steady ache. He found Brom, and held him, a breath, two, and then very carefully, with the smallest piece of himself he could spare, he let the hip lean, the way it leaned before a burst, only the first beginning of the Wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:131:The listening went out of him at once. It did not fade; it was shouldered aside, as two men trying to go through a narrow door at once leave one of them on the step, and the Wind's readiness was through the door and the new thing was outside it, gone. He tried it the other way, with the giving face, the faintest stir under his ribs, and it was the same. Whatever this was, it would not stand in the same room as either of the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:141:"Sitting still or standing still. Quiet. One strong one, close, three paces or nearer. A few breaths to open it, if I'm lucky and I don't try. A few to hold it. Then something moves and it's gone, and a minute after, the head." He looked at his hands. "On a floor, against somebody, it'd be worth nothing. With more than one person near me I don't know what it would do. In a room with a crowd in it—" He did not finish. He had a good idea what a crowd would do to a thing that could be knocked over by one big man shifting on a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:151:"It's for *knowing*." Brom said it plainly, the way he said everything, and then stood a moment as though he were checking it, and found it would do. "Not for winning anything. Knowing who's in the room. What they are. Where they are, before you've looked." He put his hand on the arch. "I've read your book. You've been finding things out for their own sake since before you came over the hills. You don't need me to tell you that's worth something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:169:*What it is: the edge of B.'s read. A thin thing on the skin, all over, outside. It doesn't go out. Things come in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:171:*Source: B. Third morning, mid-reset, not asked for. Ruling: same road as the other two. Close, and long, and a real person, and not taken. Found afterward.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:175:*Deployment range: one strong one, close, three paces or under. Opened once in about forty tries, then twice more. Held four breaths, three, five. Lost every time he moved, or I did. Gives: where, which way, that he's alive, a shape I haven't words for. Doesn't give: how far, exactly. Who. Anything moving. Anything at range.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:177:*Costs: attention itself, whatever I look with. Four breaths cost what a night of the gaze costs. A band round the head a minute after, an hour or more. And it won't share: if the hip leans or the hollow stirs, it goes. Opening it means putting both of the others down.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:181:He read the entry through: it was the shortest *Deployment range* in the book and the longest *Open questions*. That was right; that was what the book was supposed to look like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:183:There was one more thing he made himself write, because he could feel himself wanting to fold Tuesday into it. The new thing had come from Brom's read, and on Tuesday Brom's read had lost him, and the two lay side by side in his head as neatly as a lock and a key. That neatness was the reason not to trust it. He turned back to Tuesday's page, to the third exchange, and set down beside it, in pencil, only what he had seen of each.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:185:*Not the same thing. This came with a hush and a notice; the third came with neither. This lies on my skin and I can feel it lying there; the third left nothing on me to feel, before or after. This takes something in, for a few breaths, a man as big as a door; in the third I took nothing in. I was looking at his shoulder. This costs a band round the head; the third cost nothing I can find. This opens only when I'm still and stop aiming; the third came while I was moving, in the middle of an exchange. This is a piece of his read. The third was his read finding nobody there. One I have and can't use yet. The other I never had, and can't find. It stays where it fell.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:189:*I own something I cannot use. Twice before I've owned something I couldn't use, and both times the road from owning to using was a winter of mornings like this one, on stone, getting it wrong in front of somebody patient. I know that road now. I know what it costs a day. Begin.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:209:On Monday Dace would chalk the main season on the wall by the slate, and the floors would fill, and somebody would want him on their card who had not wanted him a week ago. He had three things that were not a Path now, and one of them he could not use in any room with more than one person in it. He had a friend whose whole Path was knowing where people stood, and who still had nowhere to put him. He had a partner at a lamp in the north window who had not yet decided what she thought of that friend, and was entitled to take her time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:19:He went down the hill in the morning in the full dark, with a lamp, before the frost had been walked on. The row was white and silent. His knees took the slope one careful step at a time and did not complain about anything they had not complained about yesterday, which he chose to count as a kind of permission. At the bottom of the row the baker's window was lit and smelled of the first batch. The Ironyard's side door was barred on the inside still, and he had to knock for the night man, who let him in without a word and went back to his blanket by the brazier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:43:"When something happens to you, you tell me. Then. Not at the end of the morning when you've worked out what it was. If all you've got is *something*, say *something*." He finished the left hand. "I've stood close to you for a month and the read's told me things it's never told me about anybody. If one of them's happening, I want to know it's happening while I'm feeling it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:87:"You're louder close."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:91:"On the read." Brom's eyes rested on him the way they always did, like a man's on weather, but there was something working behind them. "At a bench's length you're faint. You're a step upstairs in stockings. Here you're a man walking about in boots." He thought. "Still not on a shelf. Still moving round something in the middle I can't find. But louder." He tipped his head. "And the thing like a Wind's gone quiet. In your hip. It's been dark since Tuesday."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:99:"Does it change?" he said, coming back to his cross. "When I hit you. Does the middle move?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:"It leans. Toward your arm, a little, every time you throw. Like a crowd leaning toward a door when somebody comes in." Brom shrugged with one shoulder. "Everybody's does that. Yours does it from a place I can't see."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:By the end of the hour both his forearms were blue from the wrist to the elbow on the inside, where the returns had come up through them, and his hands had swollen enough that the knuckles had gone smooth. He sat on the bench afterward to write, and found he could not close his fingers properly round the pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:219:Brom seemed to think about it seriously, with his whole attention, as he had thought about *Does it bother you* in the alcove with the cloth half off his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:259:"And if your read tells you something, you say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:279:"It learns." He turned his swollen hands over on his knees. "That's the first thing. The gap after the hit, before the answer. It gets shorter every time it sees the same thing. Three times, and it's half what it was. So a fighter who wants the gap long has to keep showing new things, and most don't, because most fighters have three things they're good at and they use those." He thought about it. "And the second thing is that it's the wrong place to look. I looked there for a fortnight because it was the only place nobody else was looking. But by the time the middle starts, the man's already got your hit. The truth isn't in the middle. It's at the very start of the touch. Before anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:287:"It's true of me," said Keth. He said it slowly, as if he had not known it until he heard it said. Then he stood and lifted his blade, though there was nobody in the ring to lift it at, and looked down the length of it at nothing. "Thank you. That was worth the waiting." He walked off toward the side door, and stopped, and said without turning round: "Whatever you're doing in that alcove, mind the knees. A man who reads like you needs to be standing up to do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:9:He went on his feet, at a walk that was not quite a walk, with the open room on his left and his weight low and his eyes on the place where Brom's breath lived, low in the belly under the wound cloth of his shirt. Six strides. Four. He felt the crowd lean, all at once, the way a field of barley leans when the wind comes into it. At three strides he could hear Brom breathing. It was even and slow, a big man's breath, and nothing in it was getting ready for anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:The plan was three things, close together, each one new.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:13:The first was the short straight strike he owned, the one Lira had given him a year ago, and he put it into the lead forearm. Brom held that forearm loose across his front, as he had held it against every newcomer for twelve days, and it was the place he trusted most. Cael wanted it trusted, and he wanted the first answer hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:25:There it was: the recovery, the thing he had found live against the dock partner and staked the night on. Brom was soft, soft now all over, and would be until the next low breath came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:31:He did not throw, but went round instead, on his feet, to the left, keeping the fan open, and while he went round he did the second thing: a rising strike off the back hand at the shoulder, quicker than the first and lighter, a different shape so the man would have to think about it. It landed on an ordinary shoulder, with an ordinary give in it, and Brom let it land and did not breathe for it at all. There was no knock, no hold and no throw. There was only a big man's shoulder taking a boy's light blow and not caring, as a wall does not care about rain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:41:He had measured the recovery against the post first, and the post had come back. It hung on a rope, and when Brom threw it, it swung away along its arc and swung back on the same arc at the same rate, because that was what a post on a rope did, whether anybody wanted it to or not; half of the gap he had timed on that rope had been made by the rope. Then he had measured it live against the dock partner, and the dock partner had been heavy. He was a Bronze washout as broad as a door. Brom's answers had sent him a hand's breadth off his line, and a hand's breadth was nothing to a man that size, so he had been there, close, still inside, to put his fourth blow into the two beats.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:51:It came on the fourth contact. He went in low, testing the knee he had drawn on his coverage map, and Brom turned on his heels to take it on the edge of his lead foot. The foot was hard and sent Cael's shin back past him. But turning for it brought Brom round square, chest and ribs open, for perhaps half a beat, with his lead forearm out of the way and his breath already spent on the foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:59:He let the hollow lean, and did not answer it. It was harder than he had thought it would be; it was like holding a door shut against a wind, with his shoulder, while trying to do something else with his hands. He put a plain strike into the ribs instead, because the ribs were open whether the giving face was let out or not, and a plain strike was still a strike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:75:He had never thought about what it cost not to use something. He had a whole column in the Log for what each thing charged him to use, but none for what it charged him to carry while he kept it shut. He did not have time to think about it now. He put it somewhere, the way he put the faces in a crowd, and came forward again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:81:It was not a throw, exactly; Brom met his lead arm in the middle of the crossing, with the breath in and the forearm hard, and turned the arm a little on its way past, no more than a door is turned on a hinge. But Cael's feet were crossed when it happened. The turn went into his crossing step and made a spiral of it, so that his weight, which had been going left, went round, and on round, and past the place where his feet could follow it. The stone tipped. He felt the floor come up toward his right shoulder with all the slow certainty of a thing that has already happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:85:He did not ask it to, and he did not read anything. There was nothing to read; there was only the stone coming, and something under the thinking part of him that had kept him alive in the spring dropped his left hip without consulting him. The floor went short. He was half a body to the left, crooked, with his weight caught on the outside of one foot and his breath stopped halfway in, and he locked there, in the slow bright stillness, two *ands* of nothing, and the stone did not arrive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:91:He knew it in the lock, where there was nothing to do but know things. The first line of the two lines, the one he had thought would be the harder one, broken inside the first exchange, not by a choice but by a fall. He could feel the price of it as plainly as a coin missing from his palm: twice what a read burst cost, from the bottom of him, crooked. He had a moment, in the lock, to hate the arithmetic of it. He did not have a moment for anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:117:So: what was left. Not the hold. The hold was no use to anybody; by the time it began, Brom already had the hit. Not the recovery. That left the breath before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:133:It was a low diagonal from the north mark, from left to right across Brom's front, on his feet. He went down and long, with his weight ahead of him, covering the stone in three strides that felt like one. He saw the crowd's faces smear at the edge of his eye like lamps seen through rain. He saw Brom's feet, which had not moved. He saw the lead forearm, loose, and he aimed his short strike at the elbow behind it, where the arm bent, and he was halfway there and still a full stride out when he heard the breath go in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:145:He was breathing on Cael, on his weight coming, the moment it came near enough to feel. *Close, whatever you do,* Cael had said to him in the alcove; *it doesn't reach far.* It did not need to. It reached a bench's length, and a bench's length was two strides, and at Cael's best speed two strides were still a beat. The read found him two strides out, and the breath went in on the read, and by the time Cael's fist arrived Brom had been hard and waiting for it for longer than the newcomers' whole blows had taken to land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:147:And he had not breathed like that against the newcomers, because they were never fast enough to need it. Against them the read and the fist had come together, and he had breathed on whichever came; Cael had sat on the bench with his fingers on his wrist and timed the version of the man who was fighting twelve-year-olds. Somebody, somewhere, in a boat shed or a city by the coast, had tried exactly this before Cael was ever born into it. Some fast Wind with good legs had tried to arrive before the hardness, and had made Brom learn to harden on the read instead of the knock, and Brom had kept the lesson.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:153:He had committed to a low entry, fast, from the left, and on the second stride his lead foot came down across the long lead seam in the floor, the smooth line he had felt through his boot on the mark. It took his weight a hair less kindly than the stone round it, a hair slicker, and to keep his line he put more into the step than he had meant to. For one instant he was neither on one foot nor the other. He was a bridge, all of him in the air between his two feet with his weight in the middle and going forward, the one moment in any step when a body has nothing to stand on but its own speed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:167:Brom had stepped forward to take the space while he was down. He was a stride and a half away, on his heels again, the breath going out of him long through the nose, and Cael needed room. He needed to be somewhere else, now, before the next breath came in, and he read it: Brom's weight settling forward over the front foot to take the next step, the heel going light. It was a read, clean, a hinge, the first he had been given all night. He set himself over his feet while he watched it, as the drill had taught him, and when the heel came up he dropped his left hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:169:The burst fired, and he felt it go out of him at its full price, the half-breath, the whole of what it had cost on the chalk the morning of the six marks, a read burst's price exactly. And his legs gave him perhaps a third of the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:177:So that was the arithmetic, and it was very simple now. The Pressure was locked by a rule. The Wind was closed by his legs. What he had left was his eyes, and his two hands, one of them still half numb, and two legs that might last two more exchanges if he was careful with them and one if he was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:191:His knees chose the size of his steps back to the mark, and he let them. They chose short ones, and he took short ones, and found that if he kept his weight low and close and let his feet do only what his knees would sign for, the knees stopped arguing and only grumbled. He had not walked like that since his first winter. He had walked like that across the Cinder House yard to the canvas post, every morning for three months, with no hip in it at all, because there had been nothing in his hip to use. He had been slow, and careful, and he had looked at everything, because looking was all he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:197:He stood on the north mark and waited for his breath to come back into its count, and while it came he found that the arithmetic had become very simple. The Pressure was locked, by him, in ink. The Wind was shut, by his legs. The plan was dead and the second door had been bolted by some fast stranger in another city before Cael had ever heard of it. What was left was the boy who had walked to the canvas post. He had two hands, one of them still coming back from numb, and two eyes, and knees that would give him perhaps two more exchanges of short steps if he spent them carefully, and one if he did not. That boy had not had a plan either. He had not known what a read was. He had only known where his next foot was going, and what the man in front of him was doing with his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:207:He was not watching for the breath any more. He had stopped listening for it somewhere in the walk back from the floor, without deciding to, the way a man stops listening for a cart that has gone round the corner. He was not watching the purse, or the read, or the lead seam, or anything that had a page. There was only the next thing. Where Brom's lead shoulder was. Where his own left foot would go when it went. How much stone there was between them, and whether it was getting more or less.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:There was nothing in Cael that had done it. He knew that then and he would know it afterward, whatever else he did not know. Nothing had leaned toward a door, and nothing had gone out of him. He had not reached for anything; he had been looking at the next half-second, and the next half-second had turned out to have a man in it whose read was pointed at an empty piece of floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:229:It came late, and it came by sight, and Cael could feel the difference through his sleeve the way you can hear the difference between a man reading aloud and a man making it up. It was a fighter's hand, fast and trained and very strong, landing where his eyes had sent it. The turn it gave Cael's arm was real, and still more than he could stand against, and it moved him a step. But it was rough. It had been made on the spot, out of nothing, by a man who had not known it would be needed until it was. For the first time all night, Brom was building his answer in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:231:And then it was over, the way the start of it had been: without any edge he could find. Half a breath later the low breath was going in again, and the hardness was there again under Cael's hand, and whatever in Brom read people had him again, as though it had never let go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:Three hard, close together, each one new: it was the shape of last week's afternoon in the alcove, when the broad man had run four together and Brom had given him a step on the fourth, with the boy on the bench counting. Brom had paid the man a mark of his own afterward, on top of Lira's, and the man had looked at the coin as if it puzzled him. It had been for the step. Brom had wanted to see what the boy would build out of it, and now he had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:Something had gathered in the boy. It came toward the arm, slow and dense, the way a weight gathers in a man who means to put his whole body behind a blow and more. It was almost the shape the read gave a Pressure practitioner in the moment before the strike, and then it was not; it was too loose, too strange, going round that middle Brom could never find. Each time it rose to the very edge of the boy's arm, and each time it stopped there and stayed, held, the way a man holds his breath under water because the air is somewhere he has decided not to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:13:The boy had kept something shut all night, at a cost. Brom did not know what it was. He knew what holding a thing shut felt like on the read, because he had felt men do it, and it was never cheap. In the second exchange he had felt the other thing in the boy, the Wind-like flicker in the hip, go dim as a lamp goes when somebody turns it down, and stay dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:In the third the boy was very quiet on the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:21:What was left in him was the weight Brom had no shelf for, faint now, careful, close, like somebody crossing the boards upstairs in stockings so as not to wake the house. None of his touches was worth a breath, so Brom spent none on them, and waited. He had decided at the reset to see the exchange out patiently and be kind about the end of it. He had thought there was nothing more in the bout to learn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:23:He had the read on the boy at a bench's length, the way he had held it all night, laid on him like a flat hand on a beam. And the boy's weight was there under it, as it had been all night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:27:Brom had felt a great many things go quiet on the read. A man hit hard enough went quiet, sometimes, for a breath. The unkindled were quiet always, and when a practitioner covered himself, as a Shield did, the read found a hard thing pushing back against it, and that was not quiet at all; that was loud, in its way. He had a shelf for every one of those. He went along them now, very fast, the way you go along a shelf in the dark with your fingers for a book you know is there, and the boy was not on any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:29:The boy was not anywhere: the read went over the boards where the boy was standing and found them bare. They were not bare the way the unkindled were, a floor with nobody on it yet; they were bare the way a floor is when somebody has been standing on it all evening and is suddenly not, and the boards have not yet stopped creaking from the weight. There was no step, no weight, no breath, no shape at all. There was nothing there for him to be hard against.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:33:It had been two years since his body had had to decide that alone. From fourteen, the read had told it, every time, before he knew: there, the forearm; there, the edge of the foot; there. He had stopped noticing the telling, as you stop noticing your own heart. Now the telling stopped, and his body, waiting to be told, began to turn toward the last place the boy had been on the read, a little to the left, and his guard went there, and his weight began to follow it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:37:For half a beat, Brom believed the read and not his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:It hurt, and that was the astonishing thing, and he would have laughed if he had had the breath. It was a boy's blow, short and plain, with nothing behind it but a hip and a shoulder, and it went into him like a blow goes into anybody, through the shirt and the muscle and up against the bone, and it hurt the way the yard post at Velmere had hurt him when he was twelve and had no Path and no read and nothing at all but a body and a man standing over him telling him to keep his eyes open. He gave a step back off his heels. He heard himself make a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:47:It was the oldest thing he had. His hand came down onto the boy's arm by eye, the way he had been taught in the yard at home two years before there was ever a read to tell him anything; quick and plain, a man seeing an arm and taking it, and turning it, hard, by strength and timing and nothing else. It worked. It moved the boy a step. It was clumsy, too, and late, and he could feel how late it was with the whole of his body, as you feel a stair you have missed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:51:The weight was there on the boards upstairs again, faint, careful, exactly where it had been, as though it had never been anywhere else. The read lay on him as it always had. Nothing about him on the read had changed. Nothing had gone out of him, nothing had been drawn or spent or put back. There was no edge to it at either end. It was simply that, for something less than a breath, there had been a place in front of Brom where nobody was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:57:He did not trust the read any more, not wholly, not for the rest of the exchange. So he did a thing he had not done since the boat shed: he watched with his eyes and felt with the read at once, and checked the one against the other, every time, before he let either of them decide anything. It was slow. It was like reading a page with one eye shut and then the other to see whether the words moved. Every answer he gave cost him a half-beat of checking first, and the half-beat showed. Twice the boy's touches landed where Brom had been late to harden them, and they were light touches and hurt nothing, but Brom felt them land on an ordinary arm and knew the room had seen it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:59:He built a way to finish it, and then he did not use it. He could have drawn the boy toward his left, toward the open floor, where his hip was; but the hip was shut, and the boy would not go, and Brom understood that the boy was not going anywhere he was drawn. He built another: he could wait, stand on his heels and let the boy's knees run out on their own. But the boy was waiting too, in his short careful steps, and he was better at waiting than anybody Brom had ever fought; he had sat on a bench for a year doing nothing else. Brom put that one down too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:73:He thought about the shelves. He had built them out of his own body for two years, from nothing, every shape a person could make on the floor above him, and he trusted them as he trusted the stone under his heels. The boy had never been on any of them. He had known that for a fortnight, and said so, honestly, in the alcove. *You're not on one.* That had been strange enough. But for less than a breath tonight the boy had not even been the weight that was not on a shelf. He had been nothing, a place in the room where the read went and came back empty-handed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:75:There was nowhere to put that, and he did not try. He would not make up a shelf for it. He would only remember where it had been, exactly, as you remember where a stair is missing in a house you mean to live in: third exchange, a little after the middle, on the stone in front of him, under his eyes. Nothing before. Nothing after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:93:The Wind was shut, exactly as he had shut it; the hip line was no brighter than it had been after the third-of-a-burst in the second. The Pressure was where he had left it, whole and full, held back from the arm. His breath was ragged in the ordinary way, and his knees were what they had been. Nothing had been drawn out of him, anywhere. There had been nothing before it, no leaning, no hush, none of the quiet that came before a notice. There was nothing after. No ache belonged to it, and no hollow, and no thin warm line. He stood on the mark with his chest going in and out and reached inward, deliberately, with everything he had, for whatever it had been, as you reach in the dark for a banister you have used a thousand times. His hand closed on nothing. It was not that the banister was hidden. It was that there had never been one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:97:Across the floor Brom had rolled his shoulders. Cael had seen him do it, and heard the front bench see it. Now the big man stood on the south mark and looked at him, and the look was different. All fortnight Brom had watched him the way a man watches a wheel turning, interested, patient, sure of the wheel. He was not sure of the wheel now. Cael could not have said how he knew that. He only knew it, as he knew the difference between a man reading a page and a man reading it twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:107:He had perhaps half a beat to see it all, and he saw it, and found he was not even sorry. It was a good entry, the entry he would have chosen himself, against a boy whose knees were gone: no cleverness in it, nothing for the eyes to read, only the plain fact of a big man coming in a straight line at the one thing the other man could no longer do. There was a kind of courtesy in it. Brom was not going to look for anything. He was going to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:123:The stone was cold through his shirt, the only cold thing in the building; the lamps had made the rest of the main floor close and warm as a kitchen, and the crowd's noise came down on him from every side like weather on a roof. He let it come. He lay on his back now, with his arms out, and let his breath find its count, and it did, slowly. Two in and three out at first, then three and four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:143:He came without any hurry, through the noise, from the south mark, and he did not stop over Cael and look down at him the way a winner does. Cael had seen winners do it a hundred times from the east bench: the step closer, the small claiming of height, standing over the man on the stone. Brom did not do it. He came, and lowered himself, and sat down on the floor beside him with his knees up and his forearms on them, so that his head was no higher than Cael's would have been sitting up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:159:"Your pressure signature disappeared. Not blocked — blocked would still read as active resistance. It disappeared. Like you weren't there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:187:"Three hard, close, and the fourth into the gap." Brom was looking at the floor. "I felt it on the second one. I gave that man a step in my alcove last week, and you saw me give it, and you wrote it down." He considered. "It's real. The gap. I've known where it is since the boat shed. The patient ones found it there before you. So I throw people far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:191:"You saw it on the third. I watched you see it." Brom moved one thumb on his knee. "You read me right. You read me from a bench. I'm different from a bench."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:15:*Wind: read-fed bursts only. Never reactive.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:19:That was all there was on the page. Above it, on the page before, was the long ink rule he had written a week ago at the bottom of the Pressure entry, with its reasons and its *not on the forearm, not on the chest*, and above that the ceiling, and the egg and the fist and the hole. All of it was true, and none of it would be any use to him in the middle of an exchange, when he would have the time it took a fist to cross a forearm in which to remember it. Two lines he could carry. He had tried them aloud in the dark before he slept, and they had fitted into one breath with room to spare.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:21:The first line was the harder one, though it did not look it. The second was only a door kept shut, and he had kept doors shut before. The first asked him to do a thing he had never managed to do on purpose, which was to stop his own hip from answering when it was frightened. Every reactive burst he had ever thrown had been thrown by something faster than deciding. That was what had kept him alive in the spring, and it was what had cost him twice the price against Orvet's Blade. Tonight he wanted nothing in him moving that he had not read first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:23:The grey book lay beside the Log with a scrap of string in it at Friday's page. He opened it there, because he had promised himself he would read the plan once in daylight and then never again until it was over. *Three answers. Then the fourth, low, into the recovery.* Under that were the four *wrong if*s, and under them the line about Brom and the column, which he had staked the plan on. He read it all once, slowly, as he would have read another fighter's page on the morning of a bout. He found nothing in it he would change and nothing in it he was sure of, which was what a plan was supposed to look like on the morning it met somebody. Then he shut the grey book on the string and put it in his coat pocket, where it would stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:25:He thought, while he sat there with his hand on the cover, about the letter on its way to Denvash. It would be most of the way there by now, in a sack on a carrier's cart, with its list at the bottom about bread and lamp oil and *Hip: fine*. Hesk would open it at the kitchen table in a day or two and read that his grandson was looking for the boy he used to be. By then the boy would have found him on the main floor, or not, and Hesk would not know which. Cael found he liked that. It meant there was one person in the world who would hear about tonight in the right order: what he had been afraid of first, and what had happened after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:65:"Four to one," said the sister to the room at large. "That's what they're saying at the pump. Four to one against him." She sniffed. "I said to the woman with the water, four to one is what they gave on my late husband to live past fifty, and he lived to sixty-three out of spite."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:81:"You've got the face. You look like a man who's counted his own teeth." She finished the bread and stood up. "Right. You're not going to sit in that room all day reading your book. I won't have it. You're coming with me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:129:At the top of the market row Red Cap fell in beside them out of nowhere, as he did, with his cap pushed back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:139:Red Cap considered this, and seemed to find it a fair answer, and went off down the row at a run toward somebody who owed him a copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:155:"No." Cael watched the corner where the broad back had gone. All fortnight they had seen each other every day: across the alcove, at the baker's door, on the corner of the row. The man had sat a bench's length from him and told him what he saw. "He's staying out of my way today. So I don't have to decide whether to talk to him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:169:There were more than he had ever seen in the building. The fourteen practitioners' lamps on their iron brackets were all there, and between them the sweepers had hung a second row on hooks driven into the beams. These were plain tin lamps borrowed from the carters' inn and the chapel and, by the look of one of them, somebody's kitchen, so that the whole low ceiling of the main floor would be a field of small flames. Two men on ladders were trimming wicks. Dace stood under them with a coil of rope over his shoulder and told them which ones were crooked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:187:"You've got carters from the north gate betting on you," she said. "Carters don't know a Wind from a Stone. They've come because somebody told them there's a boy fighting tonight who isn't on the registry's ladder at all, against a man who's never been made to do anything." She went back to her page. "Yes. It'll be talked about. Go and eat something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:193:"The main floor's book. It doesn't go in with the rest." She went on writing with the other hand. "Every bout on this floor in forty years is in there, and some from before that, in a hand I learned to read when I was younger than you. Tonight's will go in it after it's gone in the day book. Two lines, and then the same two again." She blotted. "Twice, so that if anybody ever burns one of them, the other will still say what happened."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:223:He had fought before a hundred and sixty on a middling night, and before nearer three hundred at the Cinder House for the bout that had brought him to everybody's notice last year, which he had thought at the time was as many people as could stand in one place and look at the same thing. This was more. The benches were full from end to end, and people stood on them, which Dace usually did not allow, and behind the benches they stood against the walls three and four deep. They came in at the side door with the cold on them and stamped their feet and looked round at the lamps and said *well* to each other. Some of them he knew. The fruit woman was on the second bench with her basket on her knees and her copper on Brom. The chestnut man had moved his brazier to the north end and was doing such trade that he had sent his boy for more coal. The four girls from the wall were in a row by the water barrel, with Lira's dock partner behind them like a wall of his own. The river-academy man was three benches back with his hands flat on his knees, as always, and as always he gave Cael the small left-handed nod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:227:He did his warming-up where he always did it, at the north end, by the rope, and he did it exactly the way he always did. Ankles, knees, hips; the arms; the short straight strike thrown at nothing until it ran smooth; three bursts to the fan on the chalk, front-left and left and back-left, to know the hip was there. He had thought about leaving the bursts out, to keep the hip fresh. He had decided not to. To do anything different from the usual on a night like this would be a tell of its own, and there were people in this room tonight who were paid to read tells.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:241:Lira was at the rope by the north post when he got there, with her bad arm held in against her and her good one on the strand. She had been watching the room, not him. She read a crowd the way she read a fighter, from the feet up, and he could see her doing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:247:"It's the biggest he's had, too. I asked Vell." She tipped her head across the floor. Brom was at the south mark already, alone, with his coat off and folded on the rope post and his hands wound. He was not warming up at all. He stood with his weight back and looked at the stone in front of him, and the noise of four hundred people went round him the way a river goes round a pier. "He's not going to play to it. He doesn't know it's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:257:She stood at the main floor's edge, the book open along her left forearm and the pen in her right hand, and she did not raise her voice, and she did not need to. The lamps had made the low room close and warm, and the stone took every word and laid it down where it was meant to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:267:The room did a thing then that he had not heard it do before. It did not go quiet, because four hundred people cannot go quiet. But the noise of it changed, all at once, from the sound of a crowd talking to itself to the sound of a crowd waiting, low and close, like a held breath that has nowhere to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:273:He stood on the north mark with the open room to his left and the old foundry stone dark and dead under his feet. It was cold through his boots, colder than the side floors, as if the stone kept a winter of its own down in the thick of it and did not care what the lamps were doing overhead. Somewhere under the left heel ran one of the long straight cracks that somebody had filled with lead, and he could feel the line of it through the sole, very faintly, smoother than the stone round it. He moved his foot an inch off it, and then thought about why he had, and left it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:277:He had thought, all fortnight, that when this moment came he would feel the big man's read on him. He had imagined it as a kind of pressure, a hand laid flat on his chest from twelve strides off. There was nothing, and it did not reach so far; Brom had told him so, in the alcove, with the cloth half off his hand. From here, to whatever it was in Brom that read people, he was only a boy on a mark. It would not find him until he was close enough to touch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:279:That was the whole shape of the night, Cael thought. Everything he had learned about this man he had learned from a bench's length away, by looking. Everything the man would learn about him tonight, he would learn from close, by touch. One of them had been studying for a fortnight. The other had been waiting for the studying to come near enough to feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:285:Brom lifted his eyes from the stone, then, and looked straight across the twelve strides at him. It was not a hard look or a long one. It was the look a man gives a page he has read the first line of and means to read to the end. Then he let his eyes go back down to the floor between them, and Cael understood that nothing more would pass between them now until one of them crossed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:He stopped counting the exchanges somewhere after thirty, and he stopped thinking about the lean. He was not watching Brom any more in the way he had watched him from the bench; he was simply there, close, a forearm off, with nothing between them but the next blow and the next answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:27:It did not settle in his hip, where the Wind lived. It did not settle low under his ribs, where the giving face sat waiting. It went outward, and spread over him from somewhere in the middle and lay down on the outside of him, thin, all over at once, the way the first frost lies on a field: on his arms and his back and his face and the palms of his hands, a fine new layer of something laid on his skin that he had not put there. And through it, for one long breath, he could feel the alcove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:Not see it, but feel it: the cold dense weight of the stone wall at his back, a breath behind him, pressing very faintly on the back of his neck as a wall presses on you in the dark. The post on its rope to his left, a small hard shape hanging. The lamp's little warmth above. And in front of him, close, a forearm off, a great warm blurred weight in the air, big as a door, steady as a post. It pressed against the front of him everywhere at once, so that he could have told, with his eyes shut, exactly where it stood and how far.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:37:Brom had begun to set his feet for the next one. He stopped too, half set, and looked at him, and Cael saw him feel it: not see it, feel it, on the read, close. The big man went very still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:Note: surface-awareness component. Pressure read, limited range.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:54:He read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:56:He read it three times through, with his finger under each line as he had read Vell's book on his first morning in the district, before he would let himself believe any of it. It was a notice, the shape of the others; it had their fields, their flat voice, their *unnamed* and their *partial*, and the same bare word in the place where a tier should be. He knew how to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:58:Except for the last line, because none of the others had had a last line like it. The Wind's notice, at Torvin's, had told him a name and a duration and nothing about what the thing did; he had spent months finding that out with Lira and a stick. The Pressure's had been the same; they had always left him to work the rest out on the floor. This one had a *Note*. It said what the thing was for, or what it was made of, in six words and a comma, as if whatever kept the form had decided that this time it would tell him something to begin with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:60:*Surface-awareness. Pressure read, limited range.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:62:He sat with the pen still uncapped in his fingers and read the six words again, one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:64:*Surface* he understood, because he could feel it: the new thin layer lying on him, on the outside, where the Wind had never been and the giving face had never been. *Awareness* he thought he understood, because of the breath when he had felt the wall at his back and the lamp's warmth and the great blurred weight in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:66:*Pressure read* frightened him a little, in a way none of the other words did, because he knew what a pressure read was. He had sat a bench's length from one for twelve days while it laid itself on him like a hand on a beam. He had heard it described, in the alcove, by the only man in the district who had one, as a sense of who was walking about on the floor upstairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:70:He looked up from the page at last, at the big man standing on the cross in front of him with his hands still half set, and understood exactly whose *pressure read* it was, and exactly what *limited range* meant. The length of a bench. Close, whatever you do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:72:He had not asked for it; he had been splitting wood. He had not reached for anything all morning; there had been nothing in him reaching. It had come the way the Wind had come and the way the Pressure had come, after close days of somebody's whole body against his own: not taken, not called, simply arrived and found in him afterward, like a word you discover you have picked up from someone you have spent too long beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:80:Brom came off the cross then, and crouched, so that their heads were level, and took the book in both hands as though it might spill. He read the notice with his lips moving very slightly, the way he had read the keeper's book from the front, each word on its own, making sure of each before he trusted the next. He read it again. Then he sat back on his heels and looked at Cael, and then at the page, and then at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:84:He did not say it as an accusation. He did not say it as a wonder, either. He said it the way he had said *something I've never seen before* in the alcove a fortnight ago: a man reading a figure off a page and wanting to get every digit of it right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:86:"Part of it," said Cael. "The edge. The read, it says, and not much of that. Limited." He looked at his own hands, turned up on his knees, blue at the wrists. "Not the hardness. I don't think I'll get that by standing here. I don't think it works like asking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:96:Brom was quiet. Then he said, carefully, because it was the term and he had promised: "I had you. On the read. When it came." He seemed to look for the words, as he had looked for them for the shelves. "The weight in you that moves about. For a breath it had a thing in it that I knew. Like walking into a strange house and seeing one of your own chairs." He stopped, and decided that would do. "Then it went back to being you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:98:"Can I use it?" said Cael, mostly to himself. "That's what you want to ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:102:"Not yet. I've got it. That's a different thing." He thought of the Wind, months of mornings on the straw before it would come when he called it. "It'll be a long while. If it's like the others."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:110:And there was the other thing, which he did not like to look at directly. The man in front of him had just had a piece of himself turn up in somebody else, without being asked. If it had been Cael, he thought, he would have wanted to see the whole of the place it had gone. Not the one page. Not a careful summary read out by the person who had it. Everything, so that he could judge for himself where his piece had landed and in what company.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:126:Brom read it from the front.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:132:Brom read the six fields with their *Claim* and *Evidence* and *Ruling*. He read the Wind, the whole of it: the half-breath, the lock, the hip, the fan and the empty quarter, *It does not shorten. It is not a fault. It is a price.* He stopped on that for a long time. He read the REVISIONS, and the update word for word, and the chain of two, *the fill shows*. Once he lifted his eyes from the page to Cael's chest, the way Lira had, and went back down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:134:He read the Pressure more slowly still: the taking face and the giving face, and the wrist, and the ceiling, *egg, fist, hole*. He read *It isn't the thing that fails past three. It's me*. And then he came to the bottom of the entry, and Cael watched his eyes go along a line, and stop, and go back to the start of it, and he knew which line it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:150:Brom read the ink one. *Against B.: no giving face. Not once.* He stopped laughing and looked at it for a while, and then, without saying anything, he looked across at Cael's right shoulder, the one the quarter had rung like a bell, and back to the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:154:At the head of the Wind entry, under *Source*, there was a name, and Brom's finger stopped under it for a moment, and he looked up at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:158:"Since the start. Since before I'd written any of it down properly." Cael found he wanted Brom to understand this, more than he had expected. "She's read every page. She measured half of them. That's her stick in the margin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:162:"Good," he said, and went on reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:164:He read the back of the book, the things that were not fields. *The fragments are not static.* The farmer. *Whatever I ever borrow will come with fingerprints.* Then the eight instances carried from the old Log. He read Tuesday's page, the whole of it, the plan and the second door and the third exchange and the accounting. He read the two ledgers, and the narrow column at the edge of the page headed *Carrying*, with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:168:He did not laugh at that one, or say anything. He sat with the book open on his knees and looked at the line for a long while, and Cael, across the alcove, watched the back of the big man's neck go slowly red, from the collar up, all the way to his ears. Brom did not look up. He turned the page, quite carefully, onto the morning's notice in its fresh ink, and read that again, and then he closed the book and held it out across the stone in both hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:180:"Does it change anything?" said Cael. "Now you've read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:192:"At home, when a thing came fast, my grandmother used to say the bill came after. A good harvest, a quick promotion, a marriage settled in a month. Fast meant there was something nobody had read yet in the small print." He moved one thumb on his knee. "I don't think she was right about most things. But I've watched a lot of people in three cities, and when something comes to somebody fast, it's very good or it's very bad, and they can't tell which from inside. And neither can anybody who's only watched them a month." He paused. "I don't know which this is. I'd like to watch long enough to find out. I'd like to be there if the bill comes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:196:"I think it's early," said Brom. "I think you should go on keeping that book the way you keep it. It's the most honest thing I've ever read about anybody, and you wrote it about yourself, which is harder." He got up off the floor, slowly, a big man unfolding, and stood looking down at the cross of chalk. "Who else has read it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:218:"I know you have," said Brom. "I read it in her book on the first morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:220:"Not tomorrow," he said. "The sparring. I'll come at the same hour, but we're not going to hit each other." He reached for his coat on its nail. "I've had the read two years. Since my station. I'm the only person in this city who knows what it's meant to feel like from inside, and you've just been handed a piece of it with no instructions but six words and a comma." He put the coat on. "Tomorrow you're going to try to open it. And I'm going to stand there and tell you why you can't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:230:He sat down across from her and opened the Log to the morning's page and turned it round, as he had turned it round for her a hundred times in a year, and she read the notice. She read it with her lips not quite moving. When she got to the *Note* she read it twice, and then she looked up at him with an expression he had not seen on her since the day, a year ago, when he first turned the old Log round on a table and let her read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:236:"His." It was not a question. She put her finger on *Pressure read*. "That's his. The thing he does with you on the bench."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:246:"The whole Log," said Cael. "In the alcove. He read it from the front."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:254:"No," said Lira. "That's not right." She was looking at the lamp. "It was yours. It was always yours. I read it because you let me, and I liked being the only one you let." She folded her hands on the table. "I minded, just now. When you said. I'm telling you I minded so you'll know I'm not pretending I didn't. And now I've said it, and you can stop looking at me like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:258:"Like a man who's counted his own teeth." She almost smiled. "What did he say? When he'd read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:272:"Mine didn't come with a note," she said. "The Wind. When you got it off me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:276:"Just *Wind-adjacent* and nothing, and you had to find out the rest with me and a broom handle for a whole winter, on cold stone, while I hit you." She turned the page back to look at it again. "And his comes with a *note*." She shook her head slowly. "I'd have liked a note."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:280:"You'd have argued with it," said Lira. "Then you'd have tested it to find out whether it was lying." And then, as if she had only just heard it: "He read it from the front."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:61:He did not write it under the Wind or the Pressure. It was not about either, mostly, so he turned to the back of the book, past the empty pages, to the place where he kept the things that were not fields, under *The fragments are not static*, and the farmer, and the fingerprints. He wrote the date, and then he sat for a while with the pencil over the paper, because there was a great deal and he wanted to put it down in the right order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:67:*The second door: speed, to get there before the breath. Shut too. Against fast men he breathes on the read, not on the fist. The middle column was the man who fights newcomers. Somebody faster than me taught him that years ago. The column was true and out of date.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:69:He read it over and left it, and then came the harder part.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:71:*Third exchange, a little past the middle. His read lost me. Less than a breath. He said, after: like you weren't there. I saw his guard go to an empty place on the floor and his weight hang. I hit him once, plain, under the arm, and it went into a man. Then his read had me again.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:73:*I did nothing. Nothing leaned before it. Nothing went out. No ache after, no hollow, no line. I looked for a handle after and there isn't one. Not Wind. Not Pressure. Not a fragment: no hush, no notice. One time is a thing that happened, not a thing I have. Don't give it a name. Don't go looking for it. Write it down and leave it where it fell.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:77:*Wind: two. One off a fall in the first, reactive, rule broken inside the first exchange. Full reactive price, crooked, the wide line. One read in the second, after the knees: full read price, a third of the ground. A thing that charges whole and pays a third is a leak. Shut it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:81:He stopped there, and read that last line again, and sat a long time looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:He had a column for what every one of them cost him to use. He had kept it since the spring: the half-breath, the lock, the hip, the hollow, the wrist. He had priced every one of them down to the copper, and he had never in his life thought to price the other thing, the carrying, the cost of having a thing in you and keeping it shut while it leaned. And it had cost him. It had cost him twice tonight, in ground, on the main floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:87:*Every ability has two ledgers. One is what it costs to use. The other is what it costs to carry it shut, and not use it. I've only been keeping the first. Open the second.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:91:He was going to close the book then, and had the cover half shut, when he opened it again and put the pencil to the very bottom of the page, below the column, and wrote one more line, smaller than the rest. It was not tactics; it was the truest thing that had happened to him all day, and the book was for true things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:101:He could not do the stairs in the morning except sitting down, one at a time, the way a small child does them. So he did them that way, with his legs out in front of him and the bannister rail under his hand. At the bottom the heavyset man's wife looked at him and put a bowl of porridge in front of him without a word, and the sister began to tell him what her late husband had done for knees, which involved goose fat and a great deal of shouting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:109:"You don't need bread. There's bread." She pointed with her staff at the end of the loaf on the board. Then she looked at him again, at his face this time, and he saw her see that he was going to go anyway. "Fine," she said, and went out to the hall, and came back with the broom-handle stick, the one with the notches in it that she had measured his bursts against all year. She held it out to him. "Use that. If you fall over in the market I'm not coming to get you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:119:He went down the row with the measuring stick, very slowly, a step and then the stick and then the other step. It took him most of the morning to get to the bottom of the hill. People he knew stopped him all the way down. The chestnut man gave him a chestnut for nothing and would not take the copper. The fruit woman said she had won a copper on him and he could have half of it, and then did not give him half of it. Red Cap came up out of nowhere and walked beside him for a while, looking at the stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:149:The baker's side door was at the bottom of the row, and yesterday's bread was two for one before the chapel bell, and Cael got there with the bell not yet rung and the baker's floury hand already out for his copper. He was counting it into the hand when the light at his side went, all at once, as though a door had shut beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:161:"I know where you come from, roughly, and what's on your page in Vell's book, and what the registry wrote. I've had the read on you for a month." He looked down at the stick. "You've got my numbers in a column. You haven't got anything else. That's not even." He nodded up the row toward the market. "Can you get as far as the square?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:165:The market square at the top of the row had a low wall along its upper side, where the ground fell away to the lanes behind, and the old women sat on it in summer to shell beans. In the cold of the morning nobody sat on it at all. Cael got to it on the stick and sat, and let his legs out in front of him, and felt the stone through his coat. Brom sat at the other end of it, a body's width off, with the bread on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:175:The end was a night in the long room at Velmere, when he was fourteen. He had been sent out of it after supper, as he had been sent out of it most nights that month. But the door had not quite shut behind him, and he had stood in the dark of the passage with his hand on the wall and heard a man's voice through the gap say to his father: *The reading will want doing again. Not this year. Next spring, after the work. At your friend's station, where it can be done properly.* And his father had said, *Yes. Thank you. Yes.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:181:"The man was the beginning." Brom looked across at the pump. "He came a month after the station. My father had written for him. He had very good letters. His work was finding out where a child had gone short, and putting it right, and he was very good at it, everyone said." He was quiet a moment. "He watched me in the yard every day for a week. He watched how I stood. He made me fall on the stone and get up, and fall, and get up. He looked at my hands and asked who had taught me to hold them like that, and I said nobody, I'd taught myself, at night, against the post, since I was twelve. He wrote that down. At the end of the week he told my father I had been taught wrong from the start and that was why the reading had come out as it had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:189:He had come home from the station Iron Skin and Copper with his mother's hand on his shoulder. For three weeks after, nobody in the house said one cruel word. He was very particular about that; he said it twice. Nobody shouted, and nobody sent him to eat in the kitchen. Only the long table went quiet. It went quiet in the way of a room where somebody has just stopped talking about you, every evening, when he came in to supper. His grandmother asked about his day in a voice he had heard her use once before, to a neighbour whose barn had burned. His sister wrote from the coast and did not mention it. His mother kept touching his shoulder in passing, as if to check he was still there. And in the mornings the yard man, who had stood over him with a stick since he was twelve, sat him down with his back against the estate wall and told him to stay there and not move until he was called. He did that every morning for a month, while the house decided what to do with him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:209:He knew what the even thing would be. Brom had told him the end and the middle and the beginning, and stood them up in a row, and now it was his turn. He had thought, lying in bed with the cloths on his knees, about how he would do it. He had decided he would say it first, before he was asked, as he had said it to everybody who mattered since he came over the hills: put it on the wall between them in its own plain word before anybody else could set it down in a worse one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:"The talk." Brom tipped his head toward the north gate and the road beyond it, and the cities. "There's a sentence at the back of it, about this place. After the bit about the keeper's book. *There's a boy at Ardenmere the registry can't sort.* I heard it in the river city before I'd ever heard your name. Then I came, and there was only one boy it could be." He looked at Cael. "And I read your line in her book. *Assessed.* And I had the read on you from the end of the east bench. The word only told me what they'd called it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:229:"What does the Arbiter show you?" he said. "When you look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:233:"And what you do instead. On the floor." Brom did not look at him. "The things that flicker. I've felt them since the east bench, you know. There's a thing like a Wind that comes and goes in your hip. There was something last night that came up toward your arm, twice, and stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:245:"Does that matter to you? The name?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:257:It was not a small thing to be asked. The Power Log had been Lira's and his for a year, nobody else's. She had come into it a page at a time, the way she had come into everything of his, slowly, by being there. Hesk knew it existed because Cael had told him in letters, and that was all Hesk knew. There were things in it Cael had never said aloud even to her, and things he had written in ink that he could not rub out. To hand it to somebody was to hand them the whole of what he did not understand about himself, written down in his own hand where they could read it twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:269:"Not in a bout. Not with her book open. In an alcove, early, before anybody's down." He moved one thumb on the crust. "I've had the read on you at a bench's length for a month, and on the floor for four exchanges, and I know less now than I did when I started. One night is one night. I want to stand close to you a great many times and see what you are when nothing's riding on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:281:Brom held up one hand, and turned down a finger, as though he were reading figures off a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:283:"I use the read every time. All of it. I don't hold it back to make it kinder. Half a read gets you half the truth and you won't know which half."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:333:She turned to the back, and he watched her find the night's page, and read down it, slowly, with her lips not quite moving: the plan, the second door, the third exchange, the accounting. Her finger stopped at the two ledgers, and stayed there a long time. Then it went to the narrow empty column he had ruled down the edge of the page and headed *Carrying*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:343:He waited, and she did not say anything else. She turned the page back, and he saw her read the last line at the bottom, the small one, under the column, and he saw the same small thing move in her face and be put away. She shut the book and gave it back to him.

